    textmap:
      tutorials:
        _serializedList:
        - Key: Movement and Interaction
          Value: Use <color=#4DA6FF><b>WASD</b></color> to move around the environment.<br><br>Objects
            that can be interacted with are marked with a <color=#4DA6FF><b>blue
            outline or blue icon</b></color>. Move close to an interactable object
            and use the <color=#4DA6FF><b>mouse</b></color> to interact with it.<br><br>Not
            every interactable object will be available immediately. Some objects
            have <color=#4DA6FF><b>locked interactions</b></color> that become available
            later as you progress.<br><br>When exploring, look for <color=#4DA6FF><b>blue
            outlines and icons</b></color> to identify objects that may provide information
            or allow you to perform an action.
        - Key: Pay Attention to Toast Tips
          Value: Important information will appear in <color=#4DA6FF><b>toast tips
            at the top of the screen</b></color>.<br><br>These tips can tell you
            what to do next, explain newly available interactions, or provide information
            needed to complete the patient's report.<br><br>Read each toast tip carefully.
            Some instructions may only appear briefly, so <color=#4DA6FF><b>pay attention
            whenever a new tip appears</b></color>.<br><br>If you are unsure what
            to do next, check the available interactions and wait for the next relevant
            tip.
        - Key: Inspect the Patient
          Value: The patient's current condition must be determined by <color=#4DA6FF><b>interacting
            with the patient and medical equipment</b></color>.<br><br>Look for blue
            outlines or icons around the patient and nearby equipment. Interacting
            with these objects can reveal information that is not otherwise available.<br><br>For
            example, you may need to inspect the patient's <color=#4DA6FF><b>hands</b></color>
            or use equipment such as a <color=#4DA6FF><b>thermometer</b></color>,
            <color=#4DA6FF><b>thumb oxygen reader</b></color>, or <color=#4DA6FF><b>ventilator</b></color>.<br><br>The
            information you discover through these interactions is used to complete
            the patient's EHR accurately.
        - Key: Complete the EHR
          Value: Use the <color=#4DA6FF><b>EHR</b></color> to document the patient's
            current status.<br><br>The EHR contains several <color=#4DA6FF><b>dropdown
            fields</b></color>. The correct value for each field is determined by
            information you discover during the scenario.<br><br>Do not guess the
            values. Instead, <color=#4DA6FF><b>inspect the patient and interact with
            the relevant equipment</b></color> before selecting an answer.<br><br>For
            example, a patient's temperature may require you to use the <color=#4DA6FF><b>thermometer</b></color>,
            while oxygen-related information may require you to use the <color=#4DA6FF><b>thumb
            oxygen reader</b></color>.<br><br>The goal is to use the information
            you gather in the environment to select the corresponding values in the
            EHR.
        - Key: Gather Information Before Reporting
          Value: The EHR is designed to test whether you have gathered the patient's
            information correctly.<br><br>Some answers cannot be determined from
            the EHR alone. You must first <color=#4DA6FF><b>perform the appropriate
            interaction</b></color> in the environment.<br><br>If a dropdown contains
            several possible values, identify which piece of equipment or which patient
            interaction can provide the required information.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>Interact
            first, report second.</b></color><br><br>Using the correct information
            from the environment is necessary to achieve a perfect score.
        - Key: Document Your Actions
          Value: Recording the patient's status is only part of the documentation.
            You must also document <color=#4DA6FF><b>actions you take during the
            scenario</b></color> when prompted.<br><br>For example, if you put a
            <color=#4DA6FF><b>mask on the patient</b></color>, follow the prompt
            to record that action in the appropriate part of the EHR.<br><br>The
            same applies when you perform other important actions, such as <color=#4DA6FF><b>calling
            the attending doctor</b></color>.<br><br>When a documentation prompt
            appears, complete it before continuing. An action that was performed
            but not documented may affect your score.
        - Key: Use Medical Equipment
          Value: 'Medical equipment is not only used to perform actions. It can also
            provide <color=#4DA6FF><b>information required by the EHR</b></color>.<br><br>Look
            for equipment marked with a <color=#4DA6FF><b>blue outline or icon</b></color>.
            Interact with the appropriate device to obtain the patient''s current
            values or status.<br><br>Examples include:<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>Thermometer</b></color>
            : provides temperature information.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>Thumb oxygen
            reader</b></color> : provides oxygen-related information.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>Ventilator</b></color>
            : provides information about the patient''s respiratory support or settings.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>Patient''s
            hands</b></color> : may provide information about the patient''s physical
            status.<br><br>The exact information available depends on the interaction.
            <color=#4DA6FF><b>Use the information you discover to complete the corresponding
            EHR fields.</b></color>'
        - Key: Follow the Complete Workflow
          Value: A typical scenario follows this workflow:<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>1.
            Explore.</b></color> Use WASD to move around and look for blue outlines
            and icons.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>2. Inspect.</b></color> Interact
            with the patient and relevant equipment to gather information.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>3.
            Perform actions.</b></color> Follow the toast tips and perform any required
            patient-care actions.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>4. Document actions.</b></color>
            When prompted, record actions such as applying a mask or calling the
            attending doctor.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>5. Complete the EHR.</b></color>
            Use the information you gathered to select the correct dropdown values.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>6.
            Verify.</b></color> Before finishing, make sure all required patient
            information and actions have been documented.<br><br><color=#4DA6FF><b>Remember:</b></color>
            The correct EHR answers are based on what you discover through interactions
            in the environment. Guessing may allow you to continue, but <color=#4DA6FF><b>only
            correctly gathering and documenting the information can produce a perfect
            score.</b></color>
